goal:
A partitioned table can carry an exclusion constraint. The comparison that decides whether a child index may be attached to a partitioned index refuses outright any index belonging to an exclusion constraint, however well the two match. pg_restore reconstructs partitioned tables by creating the parent constraint, creating each child constraint, and then attaching, so a dump containing a partitioned exclusion constraint cannot be restored.

    create table idx_excl_part (a int4range, b int4range) partition by list (a);
    create table idx_excl_part_1 (a int4range, b int4range);
    alter table only idx_excl_part attach partition idx_excl_part_1 for values in ('[0,1)'::int4range);
    alter table only idx_excl_part add constraint idxpart_id_data_excl exclude using gist (a with =, b with &&);
    alter table idx_excl_part_1 add constraint idxpart_1_id_data_excl exclude using gist (a with =, b with &&);
    alter index idxpart_id_data_excl attach partition idxpart_1_id_data_excl;

The last command has to succeed. A child whose exclusion operators are not the parent's, say a with && where the parent has a with =, still has to be rejected with the complaint it gets today. One side being an exclusion index and the other not is still a mismatch, and every other reason two index definitions are held to differ must go on working.
